    "result_text": "Done. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` with minimal structural impact \u2014 **no edits to any existing method or class**, just one schema field and one small new class:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` (int32) property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets `getNamesakeCount`/`setNamesakeCount`.\n\n2. **`OwnerNamesakeCountAdvice.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, cohesive `@Aspect` (mirroring the existing `OwnerHouseholdUniquenessAdvice` pattern) with `@AfterReturning` on `addOwner` and `getOwner`. It derives the count on the way out: the number of owners with a **lower id** (i.e. registered *before* this one) whose first and last name match **case-insensitively**.\n\n## Why this design\n\n- **Correct snapshot semantics.** The count is \"existing owners *before this create*\". Because ids are `GenerationType.IDENTITY` (monotonic with insertion), `id < self` is exactly the set that existed before this owner \u2014 so the value is stable across later reads and later namesake registrations, without persisting anything.\n- **Low structural cost.** I avoided touching the large `Owner` entity (no column/getter/setter) and the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` (no method changes). The only new code lives in a tiny class whose `WMC_other` is essentially zero, so the cost formula's dominant lever (surrounding class complexity \u00d7 files) stays small \u2014 effectively one new file plus a generated-schema field.\n\nFor the test: owners `a` and `b` share first+last with different postcodes; `GET b` counts the one earlier namesake (`a`) \u2192 `namesakeCount = 1`. \u2714",
